  <p class="lead-para">
    <strong>Is pharmacy a good career in India? Yes — but only once you separate the licensed profession from the oversupplied generalist counter tier most colleges are actually selling right now.</strong>
    The honest split is this: entry pay at the retail counter is genuinely modest and colleges are adding seats
    faster than real student interest can fill them, while pay, specialisation, and export-industry demand open up
    meaningfully later — but only for people who build a real skill stack on top of the base licence. Graduates who
    treat this as a deliberate <strong>high-value skill portfolio</strong> — a specialisation, a certification layer,
    and documented clinical judgment — are the ones who actually turn a pharmacy licence into real
    <strong>high income opportunities</strong> and a path toward <strong>earlier financial freedom</strong>.
    Graduates who pick it because "pharmacy has huge scope" is the line every brochure repeats are the ones most
    likely to land in the crowded, modest-pay end of the field.
  </p>

