  <h2 id="short-answer">The short answer to "is pharmacy a good career"</h2>

  <p>
    Pharmacy is a real, licensed, regulated profession in India under the Pharmacy Council of India — not a
    dressed-up retail job with no ceiling. But "is pharmacy good" and "will a fresh B.Pharm or D.Pharm graduate walk
    straight into strong pay" are two different questions, and most course brochures blur them together until you
    cannot tell which one is actually being answered.
  </p>

  <p>
    The honest split is this: the entry-level reality — a crowded, oversupplied college market and a genuinely
    modest counter-pharmacist salary — is real and not exaggerated. The payoff on the other side — specialisation
    pay, export-industry demand, and eventual ownership income — is also real, but it belongs to people who build a
    deliberate skill stack and treat the licence as an entry ticket, not a finish line.
  </p>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take</p>
    <p>
      This is not the "pharmacy has huge scope, join now" pitch flooding college admission material, and it is not a
      warning to avoid healthcare careers either. Both miss the point. The profession genuinely rewards people who
      go in with accurate expectations about entry pay and oversupply, and build real specialisation on top of the
      licence — and it genuinely disappoints people who went in on "huge scope" alone, without checking whether
      their target college and their target role actually sit inside the growing part of the industry.
    </p>
    <p>
      One more thing this decision is not: a shortcut you can take without formal training. Pharmacy is a licensed
      clinical profession — the legal minimum is a D.Pharm, B.Pharm, or Pharm.D from a PCI-approved institution plus
      registration with your State Pharmacy Council before you can legally dispense medicine or use the title. There
      is no self-taught route into this field, so the "is it good" question has to be answered before you commit
      years and real fees, not after.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="oversupply">The seat-vacancy paradox: more colleges, not more good jobs</h2>

  <p>
    Here is the part most "pharmacy scope" pages soften. The number of pharmacy colleges is expanding faster than
    genuine student demand for them, at the same time real employer demand is genuinely rising somewhere else
    entirely.
